i strongly believe from our perspective, the best path is a careful blend: expand online booking, e-consultations and remote care where they genuinely speed up access, but back them with robust non-digital options so no one is left behind. In our area, online booking has helped triage and freed up front-desk time, but a sizable portion of older or lower-income patients still face barriers with portals or lack reliable broadband at home. To improve timeliness without exclusion, we suggest:
- maintain fast, fully staffed phone access and same-day triage alongside online options; 
- offer hybrid models where remote assessment is followed by in-person checks if needed, ensuring a safety net for those who need it; 
- create extended access slots and local GP/community health hubs with walk-in or same-day appointments in the evenings or weekends; 
- strengthen mental health routes with quick, low-friction entry points (crisis lines, brief contact in days, supported online therapies) plus timely face-to-face follow-up where appropriate; 
- invest in digital inclusion: digital help at community libraries, loaned devices or data subsidies, translated or accessible formats, and clear guidance so people can navigate systems; 
- improve integration across GP, community health teams and pharmacies so information flows smoothly and patients don’t have to repeat histories.
This blended approach should improve speed for many while protecting those who need non-digital access or extra support!
